section.para:
			WN-LMF is the usual choice for Open English WordNet and other GWA
			wordnets. WNDB remains available for a local Princeton-style
			dict directory. The examples below use the miniature
			fixtures from the module's tests; replace the paths with a full lexicon
			in application code. WordNetUsageExampleTest asserts the
			behavior shown here.

LexicalKnowledgeBase lexicon = WnLmfReader.read(Path.of("en-wordnet.xml"));
// or: LexicalKnowledgeBase lexicon = WndbReader.read(Path.of("dict"));

A WN-LMF LexicalResource may contain several Lexicon elements. Use readResource for this shape. It returns the lexicons in document order and keeps each language's lookup index separate. The single-lexicon read methods reject a multi-lexicon document with an error that points to readResource; equal lemmas in different languages remain separate. WordNetUsageExampleTest mirrors this example:
WnLmfResource resource = WnLmfReader.readResource(inputStream, "omw-multilingual.xml"); WnLmfLexicon spanish = resource.lexicon("omw-es").orElseThrow();
spanish.language(); // "es" WnLmfDependency englishBase = spanish.dependencies().get(0); englishBase.ref(); // "omw-en" englishBase.version(); // "2.0" Synset dog = spanish.knowledgeBase() .lookup("perro", WordNetPOS.NOUN).get(0); dog.id(); // "omw-es-02084071-n"

			Lookups are scoped by part of speech and fold case and underscores the
			same way the readers index lemmas. When a WN-LMF synset contains several
			Definition elements, gloss() joins them in document
			order with ; . Against the miniature WN-LMF fixture,
			the noun dog has one sense:

List<Synset> senses = lexicon.lookup("dog", WordNetPOS.NOUN);
// senses.size() = 1
// senses.get(0).id() = "mini-n1"
// senses.get(0).lemmas() = ["dog", "domestic dog"]
// senses.get(0).gloss() = "a domesticated canid"

			Each synset carries its typed relations (WordNetRelation) to
			other synsets. Follow a relation with Synset.related(...),
			resolve the returned ids with synset(id), or navigate in one
			step with related(...) on the knowledge base. Starting from
			the noun dog above, the hypernym relation leads to its
			parent concept:

Synset dog = lexicon.lookup("dog", WordNetPOS.NOUN).get(0);
List<String> parents = dog.related(WordNetRelation.HYPERNYM);
// parents = ["mini-n2"]

Synset parent = lexicon.synset(parents.get(0)).orElseThrow();
// parent.lemmas() = ["canid"]
// parent.gloss() = "a carnivorous mammal with nonretractile claws"

// one-step navigation, back down the hyponym relation:
// lexicon.related("mini-n2", WordNetRelation.HYPONYM) = ["mini-n1"]

			The expanded relation vocabulary is navigated through the same API. For
			example, semantic-role relations can resolve the participants of an event:

Synset purchase = lexicon.lookup("purchase", WordNetPOS.VERB).get(0);

String agentId = purchase.related(WordNetRelation.INVOLVED_AGENT).get(0);
String instrumentId = purchase.related(WordNetRelation.INVOLVED_INSTRUMENT).get(0);

lexicon.synset(agentId).orElseThrow().lemmas();       // ["buyer"]
lexicon.synset(instrumentId).orElseThrow().lemmas(); // ["payment card"]

			This listing is mirrored by
			WordNetUsageExampleTest.testNavigateSemanticRoleRelations.

MorphyLemmatizer implements the Morphy algorithm against a loaded lexicon and the irregular-form exception lists (noun.exc, verb.exc, adj.exc, adv.exc). Exception hits are returned first; regular detachments are kept only when the candidate is in the lexicon. Unknown forms yield the marker O.
MorphyExceptions exceptions = MorphyExceptions.load(Path.of("dict")); MorphyLemmatizer lemmatizer = new MorphyLemmatizer(lexicon, exceptions);
lemmatizer.lemmatize(new String[] {"mice"}, new String[] {"NNS"})[0]; // "mouse" lemmatizer.lemmatize(new String[] {"dogs"}, new String[] {"NNS"})[0]; // "dog"

			LexicalExpander turns a term into related terms from the
			knowledge base: synonyms sharing its synsets, hypernym ancestors up to a
			configured depth, and optionally direct hyponyms. Each
			Expansion includes a heuristic weight in
			(0, 1]: the first sense starts at 1.0, later
			senses multiply by the sense decay, and each hypernym or hyponym step
			multiplies by the depth decay. The result excludes the input term.
			Defaults use depth 1, sense decay 0.5, and
			depth decay 0.5. The example uses a two-sense noun taxonomy and
			is checked by LexicalExpansionUsageExampleTest.

// dog sense 1 = {dog, domestic dog}, hypernym {canid}
// dog sense 2 = {dog, frank, hot dog}, hypernym {sausage}
List<Expansion> expansions = LexicalExpander.builder(lexicon)
    .build()
    .expand("dog", WordNetPOS.NOUN);

// "domestic dog": SYNONYM, senseRank 0, weight 1.0
// "canid":        HYPERNYM, depth 1, weight 0.5
// "frank":        SYNONYM, senseRank 1, weight 0.5

SynsetSimilarity scores two synset identifiers on the hypernym graph. Path similarity is 1 / (1 + d) for the shortest distance d through a common ancestor. Wu-Palmer similarity relates the depth of the deepest common ancestor to the depths of both synsets, with depths counted in nodes so the root has depth one. A synset's depth uses its longest acyclic route to a root, and any shared ancestor scores above 0. Only unrelated synsets score 0. Leacock-Chodorow similarity uses the shortest path and a caller-supplied taxonomy depth. The example uses synset ids n1 to n9 and is checked by LexicalExpansionUsageExampleTest.
// entity (n1) > physical (n2) > organism (n3) > person (n4) // > scientist (n5) > chemist (n6) // physical (n2) > location (n7) > city (n8) // paris (n9) is an instance of city SynsetSimilarity similarity = new SynsetSimilarity(lexicon);
similarity.path("n6", "n5"); // 0.5 (chemist to scientist) similarity.wuPalmer("n5", "n6"); // 10.0/11.0 (deep shared ancestry) similarity.path("n6", "n8"); // 1.0/7.0 (chemist to city, six edges) similarity.leacockChodorow("n6", "n5", 6); // log(6)

HypernymTyper types a noun using the nearest registered anchor in its hypernym graph. The caller maps anchor lemmas to the labels they confer, and any noun whose senses lead up to an anchor receives that anchor's label. Instance hypernyms count, so named entities reach their class anchors. The walk is upward only, and a noun reaching no anchor gets no type. The example uses the taxonomy from the previous section and is checked by LexicalExpansionUsageExampleTest.
HypernymTyper typer = new HypernymTyper(lexicon, Map.of("person", "person", "location", "location"));
typer.type("chemist"); // Optional[person] typer.type("paris"); // Optional[location] typer.type("organism"); // Optional.empty, the walk is upward only
